# chain-route-setup.sh

*[Русская версия / Russian version](README-chain-route-setup.md)*

An additional script for the [hysteria_naive_v2](https://github.com/FrankX3M/hysteria_naive_v2) project.
Transforms an installed server into **a bridge to a second server** with geo-routing:
specified countries and domains go directly, everything else—through a SOCKS5/HTTP tunnel
to the second server. Patches **both** project entry points—NaiveProxy (`sing-box`) and
native Hysteria2 (`hysteria-server`, profiles `admin-HY2`/`test-HY2`)—with the same
domain/country policy, so it doesn't matter which protocol a particular client uses.

Typical architecture:

```
                       ┌─(NaiveProxy)──┐
Client ────────────────┤               ├──▶ Server1 (RU) ──(GeoIP/domain routing)──┬──▶ .ru / .su / .рф / YouTube — direct
                       └─(Hysteria2)───┘                                            └──▶ everything else — through Hysteria2 tunnel
                                                                                          to Server2 (e.g., foreign)
```

## What it does

1. **Configures local Hysteria2 client** on Server1—prompts to insert
   `hysteria2://...`/`hy2://...` link from Server2, installs `hysteria`,
   generates `/etc/hysteria/client.yaml`, brings up `hysteria-client.service`
   (systemd), and verifies the tunnel actually works.
2. **Patches `render_singbox()`** in the project's `tools/proxy_admin.py` so the
   NaiveProxy entry:
   - wraps all outgoing traffic in the SOCKS5/HTTP client from step 1;
   - routes (`direct`) traffic belonging to specified countries—by current GeoIP
     database (`sing-geoip`, `rule-set` branch);
   - routes direct domains from the exclusion list (default: `.ru`, `.su`, `.рф`,
     and YouTube domains—customizable);
   - determines actual domain via SNI/Host through `sniff`, even if client sends
     bare IP instead of domain (important—some VPN/proxy clients resolve DNS
     locally and send IP to server, making domain-based routing ineffective
     without sniff);
   - resolves domains for both outbounds strictly to IPv4 (`domain_resolver:
     ipv4_only`) to prevent inconsistency—where some clients get IPv4 server
     address and others get IPv6.
3. **Patches `render_hysteria()`** in the same file—with the same policy, but
   using Hysteria2's native mechanism (`outbounds` + `acl.inline`), so the
   `hysteria-server` entry (profiles `admin-HY2`/`test-HY2`) behaves identically
   to NaiveProxy: same domains and countries—direct, everything else—to the same
   local SOCKS5/HTTP tunnel from step 1. Enables Hysteria2 server-side `sniff`
   (requires version **≥ 2.6.0**)—without it, ACL by domain doesn't work for
   clients that resolve DNS locally and send bare IP (see "Implementation
   Details").
3a. **Optional—force domains through chain.** List of domains that must go
   through the second server even if matching `.ru` or GeoIP-country (e.g.,
   `gosuslugi.ru`). Rule placed in both engines *before* direct rules.
3b. **Optional—reject BitTorrent** on NaiveProxy entry (sing-box detects
   protocol via `sniff`), so torrents don't leak through the second server and
   trigger abuse complaints from foreign hosting. Hysteria2 entry has no such
   rule—its ACL can't match by protocol; disable proxy in the torrent client
   itself.
4. Auto-locates installed `proxy_admin.py` (via `/usr/local/bin/proxy-admin`)
   and NaiveProxy config folder (via `docker inspect naiveproxy`)—no manual
   path configuration needed.
5. Downloads current GeoIP databases: `geoip-<cc>.srs` for sing-box (`sing-geoip`)
   and single `geoip.dat` for native Hysteria2 (`Loyalsoldier/geoip`).
6. Patches **idempotently**—all added code in both functions wrapped with markers
   (`HYSTERIA_NAIVE_CHAIN_ROUTE` for `render_singbox()`,
   `HYSTERIA_NAIVE_CHAIN_ROUTE_HY2` for `render_hysteria()`). Subsequent runs
   fully rebuild both blocks with new parameters (server change, country addition,
   etc.), not creating duplicates or requiring byte-exact old text matching.
7. Syncs patch to installed copy (`/opt/proxy/tools/proxy_admin.py`) and calls
   `proxy-admin apply`. This command validates both configs before applying—if
   something's invalid, nothing breaks, old working version stays active, script
   exits with error and prints exact reason.

## Requirements

- Same VDS where `hysteria_naive_v2` is already deployed (`proxy-admin` command
  must be in `PATH`, `naiveproxy` container—running).
- root access.
- `docker`, `python3`, `curl` (usually already installed with the project).
- Server access to `github.com` (for downloading Hysteria2 and GeoIP databases).

## Usage

```bash
# Full setup: client to second server + routing
sudo bash chain-route-setup.sh
```

Script prompts in sequence:

| Question | Default value |
|---|---|
| Second server `hysteria2://...` link | — (required) |
| Chain type—socks/http | `socks` (client address/port from step 1) |
| Proxy address/port of second server | `127.0.0.1:1080` |
| Country codes for GeoIP-direct (comma-separated) | `ru` |
| Direct domains (comma-separated) | `.ru,.su,.рф,youtube.com,youtu.be,googlevideo.com,ytimg.com,youtube-nocookie.com,ggpht.com,youtubei.googleapis.com,youtube.googleapis.com,youtubekids.com,youtubeeducation.com,gvt1.com,gvt2.com,gvt3.com,video.google.com` |
| Domains forced through chain | — (empty; example: `gosuslugi.ru,gu-st.ru`) |
| Block BitTorrent on NaiveProxy entry | `N` |

About "Direct domains" field: entered list **completely replaces** default.
To *add* domains to default list, start with `+`: `+vk.com,mail.ru`. Common
mistake when pasting manually—losing `youtube.com` from start; then `youtube.com`
itself (including `redirector.c.youtube.com`) goes through chain while
`googlevideo.com` goes direct. Script prints final parameters before applying—verify.

`gstatic.com`, `googleusercontent.com`, `googleapis.com` intentionally not in
default: this is Google's shared infrastructure (Gmail, Drive, Play, search),
adding them sends far more through Server1 than just YouTube. Video plays fine
without them.

If Hysteria client to second server already configured and working—script asks
whether to reconfigure with new link, rather than silently modifying.

### Additional modes

```bash
# Don't touch Hysteria client (already set up manually)—routing only
sudo bash chain-route-setup.sh --skip-client

# Update only GeoIP databases (e.g., via cron weekly—
# IP ranges change over time) and restart container
sudo bash chain-route-setup.sh --only-geoip

# Revert routing to clean direct-only (doesn't touch Hysteria client)
sudo bash chain-route-setup.sh --undo
```

To change second server or exclusion list later—simply run script again without
flags; it rebuilds everything with new answers.

## Post-application checks

```bash
systemctl status hysteria-client        # client to second server alive?
systemctl status hysteria-server        # native Hysteria2 entry alive?
docker logs naiveproxy --tail 20        # NaiveProxy started without errors?
cat /opt/naiveproxy/config/config.json  # final NaiveProxy config with dns/outbounds/route
cat /etc/hysteria/config.yaml           # final Hysteria2 config with outbounds/acl
```

Live routing test on Hysteria2 side—enable debug logging:

```bash
journalctl -u hysteria-server -f
# connect with admin-HY2/test-HY2 to .ru-site and non-.ru—log shows
# which ACL rule (direct(...) or chain_fin(all)) connection used
```

Live routing test—enable debug logging and check actual traffic destination:

```bash
sed -i 's/"level": "info"/"level": "debug"/' /opt/naiveproxy/config/config.json
docker restart naiveproxy
docker logs -f --since 0s naiveproxy
# connect with client to any .ru-site and any non-.ru—log shows
# router: sniffed protocol / match ... => route(direct) for .ru,
# and outbound/socks[chain-fin] for others

# restore to prevent disk filling:
sed -i 's/"level": "debug"/"level": "info"/' /opt/naiveproxy/config/config.json
docker restart naiveproxy
```

Final IP check—from two different clients visit `ifconfig.me` / `myip.com`:
- request to `.ru`-domain or YouTube → Server1 IP (RU);
- any other request → IPv4 Server2 address (second server in chain), same for
  all clients (mobile and desktop)—this is what `domain_resolver: ipv4_only` fixes.

Quick test of both branches from client console (through proxy):

```bash
curl -s https://api.ipify.org                                      # → Server2 IP (chain)
curl -s http://redirector.c.youtube.com/report_mapping | head -c 120  # → Server1 IP (direct)
```

`redirector.c.youtube.com/report_mapping` returns IP that Google sees request from—
convenient for checking YouTube branch specifically. Error `curl: (23) Failure
writing output` with `| head`—harmless (head closed pipe). Run test from **both**
client types—NaiveProxy and HY2 profile—they go through different rule engines.

## Implementation details

- **Why `sniff` is needed.** NaiveProxy is HTTP CONNECT tunnel. Some VPN/proxy
  clients on devices resolve DNS locally and send server bare IP instead of
  domain—then `domain_suffix` rule has nothing to compare. `sniff` reads actual
  domain from TLS ClientHello SNI (or HTTP Host for plain HTTP) directly from
  stream, regardless what was sent in CONNECT—substitutes it for further routing,
  not changing actual connection address.
- **Why GeoIP, not just domains.** Some traffic has no domain (IP access, some
  P2P) or SNI hidden (ECH). GeoIP by country IP ranges catches such cases as
  backup to domain rules.
- **Why `domain_resolver: ipv4_only`, not "leave as-is".** Without explicit
  resolving, sing-box passes domain as-is to SOCKS5/HTTP proxy of second server,
  letting IT decide IPv4 or IPv6—different clients see different exit addresses
  depending on entry point. Explicit `domain_resolver` forces sing-box to resolve
  domain to A-record and pass ready IPv4 address downward.
- **Why Hysteria2 side uses different GeoIP database.** Native Hysteria2 uses
  not `sing-geoip`-files (tuned for sing-box format, `.srs`), but its own ACL
  engine with `acl.geoip: <geoip.dat path>` field—compatible file (single,
  all countries, V2Ray/Xray format) script downloads from
  [`Loyalsoldier/geoip`](https://github.com/Loyalsoldier/geoip) to
  `/etc/hysteria/geoip.dat`. Explicitly stated path (not left empty)—to not
  depend on Hysteria2 auto-downloading to working directory on startup
  (`WorkingDirectory=` in systemd unit stays unchanged) and so `--only-geoip`
  can update it same clear way as sing-box `.srs`-files.
- **Why Hysteria2 ACL has `outboundName(matcher)` syntax, not `route.rules` like
  sing-box.** Two independent rule engines from different projects—`render_singbox()`
  generates JSON for `sing-box` (`route.rules`/`rule_set`), `render_hysteria()`—YAML
  for official `hysteria` binary (`acl.inline`, rules like `direct(suffix:example.com)`,
  `direct(geoip:ru)`, catch-all `chain_fin(all)` last, rules checked by order, first
  match wins). Outbound name—`chain_fin` with underscore: Hysteria2 ACL parser
  doesn't accept dash in name, `chain-fin(all)` fails with `invalid syntax`.
- **Why Hysteria2 needs server-side `sniff`.** Unlike sing-box, Hysteria2 ACL
  by default matches only what client sent in connection request. Many HY2 clients
  (including desktop with system TUN) resolve DNS locally and send server bare IP—
  then `direct(suffix:...)` has nothing to compare, `geoip:ru` for Google address
  doesn't match, YouTube goes to `chain_fin(all)`. In `hysteria-server` logs this
  shows as `reqAddr` with IP-addresses instead of domains. Since Hysteria 2.6.0
  there's server-side `sniff` option (HTTP Host / TLS SNI / QUIC); script enables
  it with `rewriteDomain: false`—domain used only for ACL, connection goes to
  address client sent (like `sniff` without override in sing-box). If `hysteria
  version` < 2.6.0, script warns and doesn't enable sniff—update binary
  (`bash <(curl -fsSL https://get.hy2.sh/)`).
- **Why "force through chain" domains come first.** In both engines first matched
  rule wins. `gosuslugi.ru` matches both `.ru` and `geoip:ru`, so only way to send
  to Server2—`chain-fin`/`chain_fin(suffix:...)` rule before all direct rules.
  Note that many Russian services (State Services/EASIA among them) periodically
  restrict foreign IP access—you'll get a stub or captcha; not a routing error.
- **Permissions on `geoip.dat`.** `hysteria-server` in project runs under `hysteria`
  user in systemd sandbox (`ProtectSystem=strict`, `ReadOnlyPaths=/etc/hysteria`).
  File `/etc/hysteria/geoip.dat` must be readable by this user—script sets `644`.
  If file is `640 root:root` (e.g., after manual `mv` from `/tmp`), server enters
  restart loop with `open /etc/hysteria/geoip.dat: permission denied`; reverting
  `proxy-admin apply` to previous config **won't help**—old config references same
  file. Fix with `chmod 644 /etc/hysteria/geoip.dat`.
- **sing-box syntax changes between versions.** Script written for sing-box 1.13.x
  syntax: `action: "sniff"` in `route.rules` (not `sniff`/`sniff_override_destination`
  on inbound—removed in 1.13) and `domain_resolver` on outbound (not `domain_strategy`—
  deprecated in 1.12, requires `ENABLE_DEPRECATED_LEGACY_DOMAIN_STRATEGY_OPTIONS=true`
  from some version). After updating `sing-box` image in project, if `proxy-admin apply`
  fails with `FATAL` about deprecated/legacy fields—script needs updating for new
  syntax (usually sing-box directly gives migration link in error text:
  `https://sing-box.sagernet.org/migration/`).

## Common issues

**"`proxy-admin apply` says "hysteria-server didn't come up with new config—reverted".**
Check `journalctl -u hysteria-server -n 40 --no-pager`. Common causes:
`permission denied` on `geoip.dat` (see above, `chmod 644`); `invalid syntax`
in `acl.inline`—usually dash in outbound name or typo in domain.
After `apply` always check `systemctl is-active hysteria-server`:
if `activating`, server stuck in restart loop, HY2 profiles don't work, though
NaiveProxy entry alive.

**YouTube goes through Server2 with HY2 client, but direct with NaiveProxy.**
This symptom means missing `sniff` on Hysteria2 side: in
`journalctl -u hysteria-server -f` `reqAddr`—bare IPs. Check
`hysteria version` (need ≥ 2.6.0) and `grep -A3 '^sniff:' /etc/hysteria/config.yaml`.

**YouTube goes through Server2 from both clients.** Likely `youtube.com` dropped
from domain list. Check:
`grep -c '"youtube.com"' /opt/naiveproxy/config/config.json` and
`grep -c 'suffix:youtube.com)' /etc/hysteria/config.yaml`—both should return `1`.

**Logs full of `bittorrent` / `TCP error ... i/o timeout` on ports 6881, 51413.**
Some client running torrent client through proxy; foreign peers going through
Server2. Either enable reject BitTorrent in script (NaiveProxy entry) or disable
proxy in torrent client.

**Manual edits between markers disappear.** By design: any script run rebuilds
both blocks completely. Anything to preserve must be script parameter (domains,
countries, forced chain, BitTorrent), not manual `proxy_admin.py` edit.

## Backups

Before each edit, script saves backup of patched file next to it:
`tools/proxy_admin.py.bak-<date-time>` (in git clone) and
`/opt/proxy/tools/proxy_admin.py.bak-<date-time>` (in installed copy).
Script doesn't auto-delete old backups—clean them manually if many accumulate.
